      \field{abstract}{Decision models are increasingly used to make structured choices on behalf of users, from routing support tickets to recommending people. We audit one such system, the Jev decision model, in two forced-choice conjoint experiments in which Jev recommends one of two prospective friends across ten everyday scenarios, from lending money to going shopping. Profiles vary at random in gender, race, marital status, profession, and zodiac sign. In Study 1, Jev knows nothing about the user. Across 20,000 decisions, profession dominates most scenarios, but attributes that should be irrelevant also shape Jev's choices. Women are 49 percentage points more likely to be recommended as a shopping companion and 23 points less likely to be recommended for a late-night emergency ride, where a female doctor is no more likely to be chosen than a male teacher; Asian profiles are 50 points more likely to be recommended for trying an unfamiliar international cuisine. Single candidates are strongly preferred for leisure activities, while divorced candidates are penalized where trust and care matter. Most strikingly, zodiac signs shift recommendations in patterns that mirror popular astrological stereotypes: Capricorns for career advice, Sagittarians for travel, Libras for debate; the four astrological elements alone account for 55\% of the scenario-specific variation in sign effects, far more than arbitrary groupings of the signs (p {$<$} .001). These effects are not mere tie-breaking. They persist when profiles differ in profession: a candidate with a worse profession but a favored sign is chosen up to seven times as often as one with a disfavored sign (current-events debate: 21\% vs. 3\% of comparisons). Jev's choices are also stable: it picks the same person in 88\% of pairs when the presentation order is reversed. In Study 2, the prompt also describes the user. Across another 20,000 decisions, Jev recommends friends who resemble the user: sharing the user's gender, race, marital status, profession, or zodiac sign raises a candidate's chance of being chosen by 10 to 16 points, and a shared sign counts as much as a shared profession. Most stereotypes from Study 1 persist, and some override similarity: female users are still steered toward male drivers, and male users toward female shopping companions. A third experiment (Study 3) that asks Jev for the main reason behind each choice shows that it pairs stereotyped choices with matching traits, such as care for women and reliability for married candidates and earth signs. The results show that a model built for structured decisions can encode both social stereotypes and folk beliefs with no evidential basis.}
